  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a decaying \ncomplex of showers and storms across portions of Northwest and \nNorth-Central Ohio this afternoon, with that trend expected to \ncontinue over the next several hours as a weak downstream \nthermodynamic environment remains in place. Meanwhile, surface \nobservations indicate a surface cold front in Michigan which \nwill continue to move south across the area by later this \nevening and overnight. Most of the area will largely remain dry \nbehind this afternoon's precipitation, though shower and storm \nchances may increase again for a tier or two of counties across \nthe southwest (Findlay to Mount Vernon) as a shortwave tracks \nalong the front overnight. \n\nHigh pressure will build south across the Great Lakes following the \ncold front on Friday, leading to mainly dry and pleasant\nweather. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUnsettled weather will return beginning on Saturday and could \npersist through early next week. Low pressure will lift a warm \nfront north across the Ohio Valley on Saturday, before it stalls\nin the vicinity of the Lower Great Lakes. A couple of mid- \nlevel shortwaves will track east along the front late Saturday \nthrough Sunday which will result in periodic showers and storms,\nthe most widespread of which should occur Saturday night into \nSunday morning. Heavy rain appears to be the main threat with \ntall, skinny CAPE profiles and anomalously-high PWATs. \n\nThe latest upper-level guidance suggests that the active weather \npattern will continue through the early part of next week as several \ndisturbances move through or impact the area. This includes a weak \nupper-level low on Monday, followed by a shortwave and residual \ntroughing by Tuesday and Wednesday. Periods of mainly rain will \noccur during this timeframe, though can't rule out some isolated \nthunderstorms with pockets of elevated instability. Areal flooding \nconcerns will continue to increase through the weekend and into \nearly next week and will need to be monitored.",
